    Section(
      title: 'Initial Presentation',
      content:
          'A 63-year-old right-handed female is referred to outpatient PM&R 4 months after a left thalamic hemorrhagic stroke. She presents with severe, unrelenting burning pain affecting her entire right side — face, arm, trunk, and leg — that began approximately 6 weeks after the stroke. She rates the pain 9/10 and states it has not responded to multiple medication trials. She is tearful throughout the interview and tells you, "I cannot live like this anymore. The pain never stops. I have thought about ending it." Her husband confirms she has become increasingly withdrawn, has stopped eating regularly, and has been giving away personal belongings.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content:
          '- **Stroke Details**: Left posterolateral thalamic hemorrhagic stroke 4 months ago. Presented with sudden right-sided weakness, sensory loss, and headache. CT head showed 1.5 cm left thalamic hemorrhage. Managed conservatively in neurology ICU.\n- **Pain Onset**: Initially had right-sided numbness post-stroke. At approximately 6 weeks post-stroke, developed spontaneous burning, searing pain in right face, arm, trunk, and leg. Pain is constant with severe exacerbations triggered by light touch, temperature changes, and emotional stress.\n- **Pain Character**: Burning, searing, \'like acid under the skin.\' Superimposed lancinating/shooting pain. Allodynia — unable to tolerate clothing on right arm, bedsheets on right leg. Hyperalgesia — light touch feels like burning.\n- **Failed Treatments**: Gabapentin titrated to 1200 mg TID (minimal relief, sedation), pregabalin 300 mg BID (no improvement), amitriptyline 75 mg QHS (orthostatic hypotension, discontinued), duloxetine 60 mg daily (nausea, stopped after 2 weeks), oxycodone 10 mg Q6H (no meaningful pain relief, constipation).\n- **Functional Status**: Prior to pain onset, had recovered to modified independence with a quad cane. Right hemiparesis mild (4/5 throughout). Since pain worsened, she has become homebound. Cannot tolerate wearing clothes on right arm — wears loose draping. Cannot sleep more than 2-3 hours. Has stopped physical therapy because touch from the therapist is unbearable.\n- **Psychiatric**: PHQ-9 score 26 (severe depression). Reports suicidal ideation — has thought about taking all her medications at once. Has been giving away jewelry and photo albums to her daughters. Husband found her writing letters to family members last week.\n- **PMH**: Hypertension (poorly controlled — the cause of thalamic hemorrhage), type 2 diabetes, hyperlipidemia, obesity.\n- **Medications**: Gabapentin 1200 mg TID, oxycodone 10 mg Q6H, lisinopril 20 mg daily, metformin 1000 mg BID, atorvastatin 40 mg daily, aspirin 81 mg daily.\n- **Social**: Retired schoolteacher. Married 38 years. Two adult daughters. Husband is primary caregiver. Previously very active in church and book club — has not attended either in 3 months.',
