    Section(
      title: '+ Explain the pathophysiology of central post-stroke pain (Dejerine-Roussy syndrome), its diagnostic criteria, and differential diagnosis of post-stroke pain.',
      content:
          '**Pathophysiology of Central Post-Stroke Pain (CPSP)**:\n- CPSP results from damage to the spinothalamic tract or its thalamic relay nuclei (ventral posterolateral and ventral posteromedial nuclei), disrupting normal pain processing.\n- **Central sensitization theory**: Loss of thalamic inhibitory circuits leads to spontaneous firing of deafferented pain pathways. The damaged thalamus generates aberrant pain signals without peripheral nociceptive input.\n- **Disinhibition theory**: Damage to the lateral thalamic pain system (discriminative) removes inhibition from the medial thalamic pain system (affective), leading to unopposed affective pain perception.\n- Onset is typically **delayed** — 1 to 6 months post-stroke (occasionally up to 2 years). This latency corresponds to maladaptive neuroplastic reorganization.\n- Affects up to **8-10%** of stroke survivors; more common with thalamic and brainstem strokes.\n\n**Diagnostic Criteria for CPSP (Klit et al. / IASP)**:\n1. Pain develops after a stroke (within a timeframe consistent with lesion development)\n2. Pain is in a body area corresponding to the CNS lesion\n3. Sensory signs (positive or negative) are confirmed in the painful territory by clinical examination\n4. Other causes of pain are excluded (nociceptive, peripheral neuropathic)\n\n**Clinical Features**:\n- **Constant burning, aching, or freezing** baseline pain\n- **Superimposed paroxysmal** lancinating or shooting pain\n- **Allodynia** (pain from normally non-painful stimuli) in >50% of cases\n- **Hyperalgesia** (exaggerated response to painful stimuli)\n- **Dysesthesia** — temperature stimuli produce abnormal painful sensations\n- Pain distribution corresponds to the vascular territory of the stroke\n- Pain is often **disproportionate** to the degree of motor impairment\n\n**Differential Diagnosis of Post-Stroke Pain**:\n1. **Central post-stroke pain** (this patient): Neuropathic, delayed onset, corresponds to lesion territory, allodynia/hyperalgesia present\n2. **Hemiplegic shoulder pain**: Mechanical — subluxation, rotator cuff impingement, adhesive capsulitis, spasticity. Localized to shoulder. No allodynia in distant territories.\n3. **Spasticity-related pain**: Muscle cramping and stiffness. Worse with movement. Responds to antispasticity measures.\n4. **Complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS)**: Post-stroke CRPS (shoulder-hand syndrome). Swelling, color changes, temperature asymmetry in affected hand. Distinct from CPSP.\n5. **Musculoskeletal pain**: From abnormal gait mechanics, deconditioning, falls. Nociceptive quality, localized, no allodynia.\n6. **Headache**: Post-hemorrhagic headache. Different character — dull, pressure-like, not neuropathic.\n7. **Pre-existing pain conditions**: Diabetic neuropathy (this patient has diabetes — must distinguish peripheral neuropathic from central neuropathic pain). Diabetic neuropathy is typically distal, symmetric, stocking-glove distribution vs. CPSP which follows the vascular territory.',
