    Section(
      title: '+ What advanced pharmacologic and interventional treatment options exist for refractory central post-stroke pain?',
      content:
          '**Pharmacologic Management (Evidence-Based Hierarchy)**:\n\n**First-line agents**:\n- **Amitriptyline 25-75 mg QHS**: Best-studied medication for CPSP. NNT ~4. This patient had orthostatic hypotension — consider nortriptyline (less anticholinergic/hypotensive) at 25 mg QHS, titrate to 75-100 mg.\n- **Lamotrigine 200-400 mg daily**: Strongest evidence among anticonvulsants for CPSP specifically (NNT ~4 in landmark trial by Vestergaard et al.). Must titrate slowly (25 mg/day for 2 weeks, then 50 mg/day increase every 2 weeks) to avoid Stevens-Johnson syndrome. **This is the next best option for this patient.**\n- **Duloxetine 60-120 mg**: Patient stopped after 2 weeks due to nausea — consider re-trial with slower titration (start 30 mg for 1-2 weeks, take with food). Dual mechanism (serotonin/norepinephrine) is beneficial for central pain.\n\n**Second-line agents**:\n- **Carbamazepine/oxcarbazepine**: May help lancinating component. Monitor sodium levels.\n- **Mexiletine**: Oral sodium channel blocker — used in refractory CPSP. Requires cardiac monitoring (ECG before and during treatment).\n- **Low-dose naltrexone (1.5-4.5 mg)**: Emerging evidence for central pain via microglial modulation.\n\n**Combination therapy**: Tricyclic antidepressant + lamotrigine. Nortriptyline addresses sleep and depression; lamotrigine addresses central pain specifically.\n\n**Interventional Options**:\n\n1. **Repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS)**:\n- High-frequency rTMS to the **primary motor cortex (M1)** contralateral to pain (left M1 in this patient)\n- Evidence: 40-50% of CPSP patients report significant pain reduction\n- Non-invasive, repeatable. Initial series of 5-10 daily sessions, then maintenance\n- Level B recommendation (probably effective) per AAN guidelines\n\n2. **Motor cortex stimulation (MCS) — surgical**:\n- Epidural electrode placed over M1. Considered for refractory cases failing all pharmacologic and non-invasive options.\n- 50-60% response rate in carefully selected patients. Trial stimulation before permanent implant.\n\n3. **Deep brain stimulation (DBS)**:\n- Targets: Periventricular/periaqueductal gray (PAG/PVG) or ventral posterolateral thalamus\n- Reserved for most refractory cases. Variable outcomes (30-50% long-term success).\n\n4. **Intrathecal drug delivery**:\n- Intrathecal baclofen has been trialed for CPSP with some benefit in select patients\n- Intrathecal ziconotide (N-type calcium channel blocker) may be considered\n\n5. **Transcranial direct current stimulation (tDCS)**:\n- Anodal tDCS over M1. Less robust evidence than rTMS but more accessible, portable, and lower cost.\n- Can be used as adjunct to pharmacotherapy.\n\n**Recommended Strategy for This Patient**:\n1. Initiate lamotrigine with slow titration\n2. Start nortriptyline 25 mg QHS (better tolerated than amitriptyline)\n3. Begin taper of gabapentin and opioids (neither effective)\n4. Refer for rTMS trial\n5. Address psychiatric emergency (suicidality) simultaneously',
