    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a management plan integrating pain treatment, psychiatric crisis intervention, and functional rehabilitation.',
      content:
          '**Immediate Psychiatric Safety Assessment and Plan**:\n\n**Risk stratification — this patient is HIGH RISK**:\n- Active suicidal ideation with a plan (overdose on medications)\n- Preparatory behaviors (giving away belongings, writing letters)\n- Severe depression (PHQ-9 = 26)\n- Chronic uncontrolled pain as driving factor\n- Access to lethal means (stockpile of medications including opioids)\n\n**Immediate actions**:\n1. **Do not leave the patient alone**. Keep her in the exam room with a staff member.\n2. **Directly ask**: "You mentioned ending it. Have you thought about how you would do it? Do you have a plan? Do you have access to medications or other means?" (Asking about suicide does NOT increase risk — it opens the door to intervention.)\n3. **Lethal means restriction**: Ask husband to secure ALL medications in a locked container and dispense them dose by dose. Remove opioids from home if possible. Prescribe only small quantities.\n4. **Psychiatric consultation**: Same-day or next-day urgent psychiatric evaluation. Consider whether inpatient psychiatric hospitalization is warranted given preparatory behaviors. If she expresses imminent intent, initiate involuntary hold.\n5. **Safety plan** (Stanley-Brown model): (a) Warning signs she recognizes; (b) Internal coping strategies; (c) People she can contact for distraction; (d) Family/friends who can help; (e) Professionals to contact — therapist, crisis line (988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline); (f) Making the environment safe.\n6. **Document** the risk assessment and safety plan in the medical record.\n\n**Pain Management Revision**:\n1. **Stop ineffective medications**: Taper gabapentin over 2-3 weeks (not effective, causing sedation). Begin opioid taper — oxycodone is not effective for CPSP and adds overdose risk.\n2. **Start lamotrigine**: 25 mg daily x 2 weeks, then 50 mg daily x 2 weeks, then 100 mg BID. Target 200-400 mg/day.\n3. **Start nortriptyline**: 25 mg QHS, titrate by 25 mg weekly to 75-100 mg. Monitor orthostatic BP. ECG at baseline.\n4. **Topical therapies for allodynia**: Lidocaine 5% patches to right arm and leg (where allodynia is worst). Capsaicin 8% patch (applied in clinic — single application can provide 3 months relief).\n5. **rTMS referral**: Schedule left M1 high-frequency rTMS series.\n6. **TENS unit**: May provide modest relief. Low-frequency TENS over painful areas.\n\n**Functional Rehabilitation**:\n1. **Desensitization program**: Graded tactile exposure to the allodynic areas. Start with self-applied stimuli (the patient controls the touch). Progress from light fabrics to firmer pressure over weeks.\n2. **Aquatic therapy**: Warm water (92-94F) provides gentle, diffuse sensory input that may modulate pain perception. Buoyancy reduces weight-bearing pain. Start 2x/week.\n3. **Mirror therapy**: Place unaffected left hand in mirror to create visual illusion of pain-free right hand movement. Evidence for central pain modulation.\n4. **Graded motor imagery**: Left-right discrimination, imagined movements, then mirror therapy — staged approach to cortical reorganization.\n5. **Sleep hygiene**: Pain disrupts sleep, poor sleep worsens pain. Nortriptyline will help. Add sleep hygiene counseling. Consider melatonin 3-5 mg.\n\n**Caregiver Support**:\n- Husband appears exhausted. Assess caregiver burden (Zarit Burden Interview). Refer to caregiver support group. Provide respite care options.\n- Include husband in safety planning — he is the primary observer of warning signs.',
