    Section(
      title: '+ Describe the multidisciplinary team coordination and systems-level considerations for managing refractory CPSP with comorbid suicidality.',
      content:
          '**Multidisciplinary Pain-Psychiatry Team**:\n\n1. **Physiatrist (PM&R)**: Quarterback of the rehabilitation plan. Coordinates pain management, functional rehabilitation, and specialist referrals. Manages medication changes.\n2. **Psychiatrist**: Urgent evaluation for suicidality. Medication management for depression/PTSD. Decision regarding inpatient vs. outpatient psychiatric care. Ongoing risk monitoring.\n3. **Psychologist**: Cognitive behavioral therapy for chronic pain (CBT-CP). Acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT). Safety planning collaboration.\n4. **Neurologist**: Co-management of central pain pharmacology. rTMS or neuromodulation referral. Monitoring for seizure risk with lamotrigine.\n5. **Pain medicine specialist**: Interventional options if pharmacologic management fails. Intrathecal pump evaluation if warranted.\n6. **Physical therapist**: Desensitization, aquatic therapy, gait training. Must be trained in allodynia management — standard hands-on PT will worsen symptoms.\n7. **Occupational therapist**: Adaptive strategies for dressing and ADLs given clothing intolerance. Desensitization of hand.\n8. **Social worker**: Caregiver support resources, community mental health connections, insurance navigation for rTMS coverage.\n9. **Pharmacist**: Medication reconciliation critical — multiple CNS-active drugs, drug interactions, overdose risk assessment.\n\n**Systems-Level Safety Protocols**:\n- **Prescription Drug Monitoring Program (PDMP)**: Check before prescribing. Ensure no other providers are prescribing controlled substances.\n- **Small-quantity prescriptions**: Given suicidality, prescribe only 1-2 week supplies of all medications. No 90-day refills.\n- **Pharmacy lockbox program**: Some pharmacies offer medication lock boxes and daily dispensing for at-risk patients.\n- **Electronic health record flags**: Place a behavioral health alert for suicidal ideation so any provider seeing this patient is aware.\n- **Care coordination meetings**: Monthly multidisciplinary meeting (or more frequently initially) to align pain and psychiatric treatment.\n\n**Insurance and Access**:\n- rTMS may require prior authorization. Appeal if denied — document failed medication trials.\n- Lamotrigine and nortriptyline are inexpensive generics — access should not be a barrier.\n- Aquatic therapy may need specific prescription language to be covered.\n- If patient is Medicare-eligible (age 63), most services are covered. Supplement gaps with social work assistance.',
