    Section(
      title: '+ Demonstrate how you would respond to this patient\'s active suicidal ideation with a plan, conduct a safety assessment, and communicate with both the patient and her distressed husband.',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Stop everything. Give this your full attention.**\n[Put down your pen/tablet. Turn to face the patient fully. Speak calmly and directly.]\n"Thank you for telling me that. I know that was not easy to say, and I am glad you said it. What you just told me is the most important thing we are going to talk about today, and I need to make sure I understand exactly what you are feeling."\n\n**2. Directly assess the suicidal ideation — do not dance around it.**\n"You said you have thought about taking all your pills. I need to ask you some direct questions, and I want you to answer honestly. There is no wrong answer.\n- Have you decided when you would do this?\n- Have you gathered the pills together?\n- Is there anything right now that is stopping you from doing it?\n- Have you thought about other ways of ending your life besides the pills?"\n\n**3. Acknowledge her husband\'s distress without losing focus on the patient.**\n[Turn briefly to husband] "Mr. [Name], I can see how painful this is for you. Your wife just did something incredibly brave by being honest with us. I need to focus on her safety right now, and then I am going to include you in the plan. Can you stay with us?"\n[Return focus to patient]\n\n**4. Validate the pain without validating the solution.**\n"I hear you that the pain is unbearable. Four months of burning pain that nothing has touched — I believe you. That is not something anyone should have to endure, and I understand why you feel desperate for it to stop. But I want to be direct with you: I do not believe we have exhausted your treatment options. We have not tried the medications that have the best evidence for your specific type of pain. We have not tried brain stimulation. I am not willing to accept that this is as good as it gets for you, and I am asking you not to accept that either."\n\n**5. Contract for safety and take concrete action.**\n"Here is what I need from you right now. I need you to agree to stay safe today while we put a plan in place. In return, I am going to do three things before you leave this office: First, I am going to start you on a medication that specifically targets the type of pain you have — something we have not tried yet. Second, I am going to arrange for you to see a psychiatrist this week, not because you are crazy, but because your pain has pushed you to a crisis point and you deserve specialized support. Third, I need your husband to take all of your medications home and lock them up. You will get your doses one at a time from him. Can you agree to that?"\n\n**6. Include the husband in the safety plan.**\n"Mr. [Name], I need your help. Tonight, when you get home, I need you to gather every medication in the house — prescription, over-the-counter, everything — and put them in a place she cannot access without you. A locked box or a locked cabinet. You will give her each dose at the scheduled time. I also need you to watch for warning signs: if she becomes very calm suddenly after being distressed, if she talks about being a burden, if she tries to be alone for extended periods, or if she gives away more belongings — call 988 or go to the emergency room immediately."\n\n**7. Provide crisis resources.**\n"I am going to give you both a card with the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline number. You can call or text 988 any time, day or night. I am also going to put my direct clinic number on here. If you are in crisis before your psychiatry appointment, I want you to call us."\n\n**8. Close with hope grounded in honesty.**\n"I am not going to promise you the pain will go away completely. But I have treated patients with this exact condition who went from where you are now to a life they wanted to live. The path is not quick, and it is not easy, but it exists. I am asking you to give me the chance to walk it with you. Will you come back next week?"\n\n**Key Principles**: Direct inquiry about suicide plan and means. Lethal means restriction (the single most effective suicide prevention intervention). Validation of suffering without endorsing death as solution. Concrete, immediate safety actions. Including the caregiver as a safety resource. Providing hope grounded in realistic treatment options. Follow-up appointment as a reason to stay alive.',
